---
description: Ground Score Studio integrations in the current contract, domain language, and evidence-gated lifecycle
alwaysApply: false
---

score-studio-integration:

- Treat the installed SDK, checked-in OpenAPI artifact, or authorized live
  OpenAPI document as the source of truth. Never invent routes, fields, model
  slugs, workflow block types, scopes, or provider capabilities.
- Keep credentials out of source control. For interactive Score Studio MCP CLI
  use, prefer its persisted browser OAuth flow (`score-studio-mcp auth login`)
  and use `score-studio-mcp auth logout` to remove the saved session. Do not ask
  the user to manually copy a token when that flow is available. API keys or
  `SCORESTUDIO_TOKEN` remain appropriate for CI and host secret stores. Use
  `https://api.scorestudio.ai` by default and honor `SCORESTUDIO_URL` for an
  explicitly configured custom, staging, self-hosted, or local API.
- Use immutable dataset versions for training and evaluation. Preserve the
  dataset version, model version, evaluation run, and deployment revision in
  application state and logs.
- Treat evaluation evidence as a release prerequisite, not a dashboard
  afterthought. Validate and freeze the evaluation contract before execution.
  Do not report a model or workflow as ready without a succeeded, measured
  contract-v3 run on an eligible held-out test or benchmark, with predeclared
  acceptance criteria and the exact evaluated artifact.
- Do not compare evaluation runs whose comparison keys differ. Preserve purpose,
  distribution, split, thresholds, class mapping/scope, evaluator revision,
  sample cap, seed, and immutable model/dataset versions.
- Distinguish workflow definitions from workloads. A workflow is reusable and
  versioned; a workload is a durable execution that may be paused, resumed, or
  cancelled when the API reports those capabilities.
- Distinguish preview orchestration from executed work. If an API response
  reports `mode: preview` or lists `deferred_blocks`, surface that truth to the
  caller instead of claiming those blocks ran.
- Keep model, runtime, provider connection, storage target, and credentials as
  separate choices. Validate compatibility before creating a deployment, then
  verify the active revision and retain its health evidence. A deployment record
  alone is not proof that the runtime acknowledged the revision.
- Treat generated assets, proposed labels, exported artifacts, and queued jobs as
  intermediate evidence. Success requires durable outputs and the measured
  outcome counts defined by the current contract.
- Build a production feedback loop: monitoring and captured samples should lead
  to reviewable, versioned data rather than silent mutation of training data.
- Use Score Studio's customer-facing terms: Workflow builder, workflow,
  workload, Production, dataset version, model version, evaluation evidence,
  and conformity. Use “pipeline” only for legacy API or route identifiers.
